\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Audio deepfake detection research has concentrated almost exclusively on
high-resource languages such as English, leaving open whether detectors
that perform well against one language and one family of neural vocoders
generalize to an unseen language synthesized with a different architecture
entirely. We extend a Mel-Spectrogram/Xception convolutional neural network
(CNN) detection pipeline, previously validated on the English WaveFake
benchmark (117,985 utterances spanning seven GAN-based vocoders), to the
Bengali BanglaFake dataset (25,520 utterances synthesized with a VITS-based
end-to-end text-to-speech system). Because VITS is architecturally distinct
from every vocoder present in WaveFake, evaluating transfer between the two
datasets probes generalization across language and synthesis paradigm
simultaneously, even though only a single Bengali vocoder is available. We
first confirm, in-language, whether Mel-Spectrogram features continue to
outperform Linear Frequency Cepstral Coefficients (LFCC) for CNN-based
Bengali deepfake detection, replicating the representational advantage
previously observed on English audio. We then measure zero-shot transfer
in both directions (English-to-Bengali and Bengali-to-English, including a
per-vocoder breakdown) and assess whether adversarial robustness acquired
via Fast Gradient Sign Method (FGSM) and Projected Gradient Descent (PGD)
adaptive adversarial training on one language transfers to the other.
\TODO{Summarize headline results once experiments are run, e.g.\ ``Results
show that Mel-Spectrogram's representational advantage persists across
languages, that zero-shot transfer degrades performance by X\% EER, and
that adversarially trained robustness transfers only partially (Y\% of the
in-domain robustness gain).''} Our study provides the first cross-lingual,
adversarially evaluated benchmark for Bengali audio deepfake detection.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
audio deepfake detection, cross-lingual generalization, adversarial
robustness, Mel-Spectrogram, Xception, Bengali speech, low-resource
language, convolutional neural networks
\end{IEEEkeywords}

% =============================================================================
\section{Introduction}
% =============================================================================

Neural vocoders and end-to-end text-to-speech (TTS) systems now generate
speech that is frequently indistinguishable from genuine human recordings
\cite{kong2020hifigan,kumar2019melgan,prenger2019waveglow,yamamoto2020pwg}.
This capability has been misused for voice-impersonation fraud and
disinformation, motivating a large body of work on automatic audio deepfake
detection \cite{todisco2019asvspoof,frank2021wavefake}. That body of work,
however, is overwhelmingly built around English-language benchmarks.
Bengali, spoken natively by over 230 million people, has almost no
detection research behind it: BanglaFake \cite{fahad2025banglafake} was
the first dataset to make Bengali deepfake audio available at scale, and
``Zero-Shot to Zero-Lies'' \cite{samu2025zeroshot} was the first paper to
benchmark detection models on it, reporting a best zero-shot result of
53.80\% accuracy / 46.20\% EER (Wav2Vec2-XLSR-53) and a best fine-tuned
result of 79.17\% accuracy / 24.35\% EER (ResNet18).

Two questions remain open after that benchmark, and the authors of
\cite{samu2025zeroshot} explicitly name both as future work: (1) whether
detectors trained on high-resource languages generalize to Bengali, or
vice versa, and (2) whether such detectors are robust to adversarial
perturbation. Separately, our own prior work \cite{ahmed2026melspec}
showed, on the English WaveFake dataset, that dense Mel-Spectrogram
features give a CNN-based Xception detector a substantial and consistent
advantage over LFCC (avg. EER 0.034 vs.\ 0.053 across seven vocoders in
leave-one-out cross-validation), and that adaptive adversarial training
against FGSM/PGD attacks recovers most of the robustness lost under
white-box attack (FGSM EER 0.70 $\rightarrow$ 0.107; PGD EER
0.90 $\rightarrow$ 0.154).

This paper connects these two lines of work. We take the exact
Mel-Spectrogram/Xception/adversarial-training pipeline validated in
\cite{ahmed2026melspec} and apply it to BanglaFake, structured around
three questions:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{RQ1 (in-language):} Does the Mel-Spectrogram-over-LFCC
advantage observed on English audio replicate on Bengali audio?
\item \textbf{RQ2 (cross-lingual transfer):} How much does detection
performance degrade under zero-shot transfer between English
(GAN-vocoder) and Bengali (VITS) audio, in both directions?
\item \textbf{RQ3 (adversarial transfer):} Does adversarial robustness
acquired through training on one language/architecture transfer to the
other, or must it be learned independently per language?
\end{itemize}

Because BanglaFake's synthetic audio comes from a single VITS-based
system --- an end-to-end flow/VAE architecture, unlike any of WaveFake's
seven GAN-based vocoders --- the English$\leftrightarrow$Bengali transfer
experiment is simultaneously a cross-lingual \emph{and}
cross-architecture generalization test, which we argue is a meaningful
proxy for real-world deployment even without additional Bengali TTS
backbones.

Our contributions are:
\begin{enumerate}
\item The first replication of the Mel-Spectrogram vs.\ LFCC CNN
representation comparison on Bengali audio deepfake detection.
\item The first cross-lingual, cross-architecture zero-shot transfer
study between an English GAN-vocoder benchmark (WaveFake) and a Bengali
end-to-end TTS benchmark (BanglaFake).
\item The first study of adversarial robustness transfer for Bengali
audio deepfake detection, quantifying how much FGSM/PGD-trained
robustness carries across languages versus how much must be re-learned
in-domain.
\end{enumerate}

% =============================================================================
\section{Related Work}
% =============================================================================

\subsection{Audio Deepfake Datasets and Benchmarks}
ASVspoof \cite{todisco2019asvspoof} and the ADD challenge series
established standardized English/Chinese benchmarks for spoofing
countermeasures. WaveFake \cite{frank2021wavefake} extended this with
117,985 utterances across seven neural vocoder architectures derived from
LJSpeech \cite{ito2017ljspeech}, enabling systematic leave-one-out
cross-vocoder evaluation. BanglaFake \cite{fahad2025banglafake} is, to our
knowledge, the first public Bengali deepfake audio dataset, pairing
12,260 real utterances (SUST TTS Corpus and Mozilla Common Voice) with
13,260 utterances synthesized by a VITS-based TTS model
\cite{kim2021vits} trained on the SUST corpus. Its authors evaluate
dataset quality via Mean Opinion Score and t-SNE visualization of MFCCs
rather than training a detector. \cite{samu2025zeroshot} closed that gap
with the first Bengali deepfake \emph{detection} benchmark, evaluating
zero-shot pretrained models (Wav2Vec2-XLSR-53, Whisper, PANNsCNN14,
WavLM, Audio Spectrogram Transformer) and fine-tuned architectures
(Wav2Vec2-Base, LCNN, LCNN-Attention, ResNet18, ViT-B16, CNN-BiLSTM),
with ResNet18 as the strongest fine-tuned model.

\subsection{Feature Representations for CNN-Based Detection}
LFCC paired with Gaussian Mixture Model classifiers has been the
long-standing default for spoofing countermeasures
\cite{frank2021wavefake}, but whether that advantage holds for deep CNNs
was, until \cite{ahmed2026melspec}, unexplored. That work showed
Mel-Spectrogram's dense 128-bin time-frequency structure transfers better
into an ImageNet-pretrained Xception CNN \cite{chollet2017xception} than
LFCC's sparse 20-coefficient cepstral representation, yielding a
consistent 33--35\% relative EER improvement across all seven WaveFake
vocoders. Whether this advantage is specific to GAN-vocoder artifacts or
generalizes to a different synthesis paradigm (VITS) and a different
language (Bengali) is a central question of the present paper.

\subsection{Cross-Lingual Generalization}
Cross-lingual generalization for deepfake detection is comparatively
under-studied; most existing evidence comes from cross-lingual TTS
transfer-learning literature rather than detection literature directly.
\cite{samu2025zeroshot} and \cite{fahad2025banglafake} both flag this as
an open problem for Bengali specifically. We are not aware of prior work
that measures zero-shot transfer of a CNN-based detector between an
English GAN-vocoder benchmark and a Bengali end-to-end TTS benchmark.

\subsection{Adversarial Robustness}
FGSM \cite{goodfellow2015fgsm} and PGD \cite{madry2018pgd} are standard
white-box gradient attacks used to stress-test spoofing countermeasures.
\cite{kawa2023lcnn} showed FGSM can degrade a lightweight LCNN detector
from EER 0.022 to 0.785, and that adversarial training recovers most of
that loss at a moderate clean-accuracy cost. \cite{ahmed2026melspec}
reproduced this pattern with a larger, ImageNet-pretrained Xception
model using an adaptive adversarial training scheme with momentum-weighted
attack sampling. No prior work has examined adversarial robustness for
Bengali audio deepfake detection, nor whether robustness learned on one
language transfers to another --- both addressed here.

% =============================================================================
\section{Datasets}
% =============================================================================

\subsection{WaveFake (English)}
WaveFake \cite{frank2021wavefake} contains 117,985 utterances
(13,100 real, 104,885 synthetic) derived from the single-speaker LJSpeech
corpus \cite{ito2017ljspeech}, covering seven vocoders: MelGAN,
MelGAN-Large, Parallel WaveGAN, Multi-Band MelGAN, Full-Band MelGAN,
HiFi-GAN, and WaveGlow, plus a full TTS pipeline. All seven vocoders are
GAN-based or GAN-adjacent flow models trained to mimic a single female
English speaker.

\subsection{BanglaFake (Bengali)}
BanglaFake \cite{fahad2025banglafake} contains 25,520 utterances: 12,260
real (10,000 from the SUST TTS Corpus, 2,260 from Mozilla Common Voice
across five speakers) and 13,260 synthetic, all generated by a single
VITS-based \cite{kim2021vits} TTS model trained from scratch on the SUST
corpus and representing a single male synthetic speaker. Real and
synthetic audio are both distributed as 22{,}050~Hz WAV clips of
6--7~seconds in LJSpeech-style directory format.

\subsection{Cross-Dataset Alignment}
\label{sec:alignment}
To make cross-dataset transfer experiments valid, all audio from both
datasets is normalized to an identical representation before feature
extraction: resampled to 16~kHz mono, silences longer than 2~s at a
40~dB threshold trimmed, and duration normalized to 4~s (padded or
truncated), matching the preprocessing protocol of
\cite{ahmed2026melspec}. Table~\ref{tab:datasets} summarizes both
datasets after this normalization.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\caption{Dataset summary after cross-dataset alignment (Section~\ref{sec:alignment})}
\label{tab:datasets}
\centering
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
& \textbf{WaveFake} & \textbf{BanglaFake} \\
\midrule
Language & English & Bengali \\
Real utterances & 13,100 & 12,260 \\
Synthetic utterances & 104,885 & 13,260 \\
Synthesis architectures & 7 (GAN-based) & 1 (VITS, end-to-end) \\
Speaker(s), synthetic & 1 female & 1 male \\
Source corpus & LJSpeech & SUST TTS Corpus \\
Original sample rate & 22,050~Hz & 22,050~Hz \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

% =============================================================================
\section{Methodology}
% =============================================================================

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.92\textwidth]{methodology_pipeline_diagram.png}
\caption{End-to-end methodology. Both datasets are preprocessed identically
(Section~\ref{sec:alignment}) before splitting into parallel LFCC and
Mel-Spectrogram feature-extraction pipelines; example feature maps are
shown for illustration (not derived from real audio). Both pipelines feed
an Xception CNN trained in two phases. The adversarial path (right) uses
FGSM/PGD attacks and adaptive adversarial training to fine-tune the same
model weights, and both clean and adversarial paths are scored with
Accuracy and Equal Error Rate (EER).}
\label{fig:pipeline}
\end{figure*}

Figure~\ref{fig:pipeline} illustrates the full pipeline described in this
section.

\subsection{Preprocessing}
As described in Section~\ref{sec:alignment}, both datasets are brought to
a shared 16~kHz, 4-second, silence-trimmed representation prior to
feature extraction.

\subsection{Feature Extraction}
Two parallel feature pipelines are used, identical to
\cite{ahmed2026melspec}, both sharing Short-Time Fourier Transform (STFT)
parameters ($n_{fft}=2048$, hop$=512$).

\textbf{LFCC pipeline:} linear-frequency filterbank (20 filters,
0--8000~Hz) $\rightarrow$ Discrete Cosine Transform $\rightarrow$ 20
cepstral coefficients $\rightarrow$ first/second derivatives, yielding a
$(60,\text{time})$ representation resized to $299\times299\times3$ for
CNN input.

\textbf{Mel-Spectrogram pipeline:} mel-scaled filterbank
($n_{mels}=128$) $\rightarrow$ log-power compression (no DCT, preserving
dense spectral information) $\rightarrow$ first/second derivatives,
yielding a $(3,128,\text{time})$ tensor resized to $299\times299\times3$.

\subsection{Model Architecture}
We use an ImageNet-pretrained Xception CNN ($\sim$23M parameters)
\cite{chollet2017xception} with a binary classification head, trained in
two phases: Phase~1 trains only the final layer (Adam, $lr{=}10^{-4}$);
Phase~2 fine-tunes the entire network at the same learning rate.
Cross-entropy loss uses label smoothing (0.1); training uses batch size
128 and mixed-precision (FP16), matching \cite{ahmed2026melspec}.

\subsection{Adversarial Attacks}
We evaluate two white-box gradient attacks. FGSM
\cite{goodfellow2015fgsm} perturbs an input $x$ as
$\delta = \epsilon \cdot \mathrm{sign}(\nabla_x \mathcal{L})$, with
$\epsilon \in \{0.0005, 0.00075, 0.001\}$. PGD \cite{madry2018pgd}
iterates this perturbation over 10 steps with step size
$\alpha = \epsilon/5$ and $\epsilon \in \{0.1, 0.15, 0.20\}$, projecting
back onto the $\epsilon$-ball after each step.

\subsection{Adaptive Adversarial Training}
Following \cite{ahmed2026melspec}, we fine-tune for 10 epochs at
$lr{=}5{\times}10^{-5}$ with dynamic attack sampling: at each step, FGSM
or PGD is sampled with probability weighted by a momentum-smoothed
running estimate of each attack's recent success rate
($m{=}0.2$), while a fixed fraction $p{=}1/3$ of minibatches use clean
(unattacked) samples to preserve baseline accuracy.

% =============================================================================
\section{Experimental Design}
% =============================================================================

\subsection{Experiment 1 --- In-Language Baseline (RQ1)}
Xception is trained and evaluated on BanglaFake alone (80/20 split),
separately for LFCC and Mel-Spectrogram features, replicating the
ablation of \cite{ahmed2026melspec} on Bengali audio. Results are
compared against the ResNet18 baseline (79.17\% accuracy / 24.35\% EER)
from \cite{samu2025zeroshot}.

\subsection{Experiment 2 --- Cross-Lingual / Cross-Architecture Zero-Shot Transfer (RQ2)}
Two directions are evaluated, for both LFCC and Mel-Spectrogram features:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{EN$\rightarrow$BN:} train on WaveFake (all 7 vocoders),
zero-shot test on BanglaFake.
\item \textbf{BN$\rightarrow$EN:} train on BanglaFake, zero-shot test on
each WaveFake vocoder individually (per-vocoder breakdown, as in
\cite{ahmed2026melspec}'s Table~I).
\end{itemize}
We additionally report a few-shot fine-tuning variant, adapting the
source-trained model on a small ($\leq$10\%) slice of target-domain data,
to characterize how quickly cross-lingual performance recovers with
minimal target-language supervision.

\subsection{Experiment 3 --- Cross-Lingual Adversarial Robustness (RQ3)}
Using Mel-Spectrogram features (per Experiment~1/2 results, assuming Mel
remains the stronger representation), we compare:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Transferred robustness:} adversarially train (FGSM/PGD,
adaptive) on the source language, evaluate clean/FGSM/PGD EER on the
\emph{target} language, zero-shot.
\item \textbf{In-domain robustness:} adversarially train and evaluate
directly on the target language.
\end{itemize}
The gap between these two conditions quantifies how much adversarial
robustness is language/architecture-specific versus general.

\subsection{Evaluation Metrics}
We report classification accuracy and Equal Error Rate (EER), computed
from the ROC curve as the operating point where false positive rate
equals false negative rate, consistent with
\cite{ahmed2026melspec,samu2025zeroshot}.

