% =============================================================================
\section{Limitations and Future Work}
% =============================================================================
This study is deliberately scoped to BanglaFake's existing single-vocoder
(VITS), single-speaker synthetic set, which allows us to test
cross-lingual and cross-architecture transfer but not cross-\emph{vocoder}
generalization within Bengali itself, unlike WaveFake's seven-vocoder
leave-one-out design. Extending BanglaFake with additional Bengali TTS
backbones (e.g., a Bengali FastSpeech2 or Tacotron2 system) would enable
a more direct within-language generalization study and is left to future
work. We additionally restrict adversarial evaluation to white-box
gradient attacks (FGSM, PGD); black-box, perceptual, and physical-world
attacks are not evaluated. Finally, both datasets synthesize a single
speaker per language, so speaker-diversity effects on cross-lingual
transfer are not isolated here.

% =============================================================================
\section{Conclusion}
% =============================================================================
We extended a Mel-Spectrogram/Xception CNN audio deepfake detection
pipeline, previously validated on English WaveFake, to the Bengali
BanglaFake dataset, and evaluated it along three axes left open by prior
Bengali deepfake detection work: in-language feature representation,
cross-lingual/cross-architecture zero-shot transfer, and cross-lingual
adversarial robustness transfer. \TODO{One or two closing sentences
summarizing the actual findings once experiments are complete.} This work
provides the first cross-lingual, adversarially evaluated benchmark for
Bengali audio deepfake detection and a reusable methodology for extending
it to additional low-resource languages and synthesis systems.

